  What your missing is that both weapons get any generic combat bonuses.
Str:18 gives +2 damage bonus per hit.  Str:25 gives +6 damage bonus per
hit.   A +1 ring of increase damage would be applied to each hit.


  Also, each weapon can be enchanted independently, allowing a total of
+14 damage due to enchantment to be safely reached, as opposed to +7 for
single-weaponing.

  Your analysis of the skill bonus damage is spot on.  Your analysis of
the to-hit bonus is good, but neglects all of the other factors that
affect to-hit chances, e.g. luck, enchantment, intrinsic to-hit bonus
for some artifact weapons, etc.

  You also missed that if the primary weapon hits the secondary weapon
automatically hits.  Think of it as roughly doubling the chances of a
hit, since the average damage of any single attack is roughly doubled.
(NB: Average is what we expect.  DYWYPI? is what we get.)

  Note that improving from unskilled to basic in #twoweaponing is
fraught with danger, due to the horrid to-hit and damage bonuses.  The
-3 damage bonus to each hit totals to -6 overall.  This is a DevTeam
deathtrap.

  To improve learning #twoweapon skill 
   - practice vs weak monsters, 
   - use a dagger as primary weapon for its intrinsic +2 to hit
     - If you don't hit you don't exercise the skill.
   - wait until you have 
     - high luck and/or 
     - highly enchanted primary weapon.
     - artifact primary weapon with to-hit bonus 
       - be sure to use it vs appropriate monsters if limited effect.

  You made a good start.  It took me months of effort and reading on
RGRN to reach my current state of ignorance and confusion about weapon
to-hit and damage.




